1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,891
This is STAT 331, video 1, part b. The topic is covariance and correlation.

2
00:00:07,341 --> 00:00:13,143
This part prepares you for two question types, each with a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:13,593 --> 00:00:22,297
Type C: find the variance of a sum or a difference with covariances. The exercise on slide 18 is of this type.

4
00:00:22,747 --> 00:00:30,747
Type D: compute the sample covariance and the correlation coefficient r, from data or from computer output.

5
00:00:31,197 --> 00:00:37,939
Part a covered types A and B: the rules for the mean and the variance, and the sample mean and variance.

6
00:00:38,389 --> 00:01:01,770
Section 1 defines the covariance. It measures how two random variables change together. The covariance of X and Y is the expectation of X minus its mean, times Y minus its mean. As an example, take the first 5 people as a whole population. Pick one person at random, each with probability one fifth.

7
00:01:02,220 --> 00:01:26,199
The purple lines are the means. The head sizes add to 20465, so the mean of X is 4093. The mean of Y is 1406.8. The lines cut the plane into four parts. Top right, both deviations are positive. Bottom left, both are negative. In the other two parts, the signs differ.

8
00:01:26,649 --> 00:01:40,174
Each product of deviations is the area of a rectangle from the mean point to the data point. Same signs give a positive product, in blue. Opposite signs give a negative product, in red.

9
00:01:40,624 --> 00:01:47,941
Here are the five products. Four are positive. One is negative: minus 12062.4.

10
00:01:48,391 --> 00:02:07,485
The covariance is the average product. The sum is 133363. Divide by 5: the covariance is 26672.6. It is positive, so a larger head usually goes with a heavier brain.

11
00:02:07,935 --> 00:02:13,929
The slide gives a second form of the covariance. Line 1: start from the definition.

12
00:02:14,379 --> 00:02:26,902
Line 2: expand the product. The grid shows the four terms: X Y, minus X times the mean of Y, minus the mean of X times Y, and plus the product of the means.

13
00:02:27,352 --> 00:02:38,040
Line 3: take the expectation with linearity. The means are constants. So the three marked terms become minus, minus, plus the product of the means.

14
00:02:38,490 --> 00:02:41,263
Line 4: the last two terms cancel.

15
00:02:41,713 --> 00:02:49,820
So the covariance equals the expectation of X Y, minus the expectation of X times the expectation of Y.

16
00:02:50,270 --> 00:03:08,873
Check it on the 5 people. The products x i y i add to 28923525. Divide by 5: the expectation of X Y is 5784705.

17
00:03:09,323 --> 00:03:27,925
Subtract 4093 times 1406.8, which is 5758032.4. The result is 26672.6. Both forms give the same covariance.

18
00:03:28,375 --> 00:03:39,021
Property 1. The covariance of X with itself is the variance of X. The rectangle has two equal sides. So it is a square of the deviation.

19
00:03:39,471 --> 00:04:00,847
Property 2. The covariance of a Y plus c with b X plus d is a b times the covariance of X and Y. The factors stretch the sides of each rectangle, so the area is multiplied by a b. A shift moves each point and its mean by the same amount. So the deviations do not change.

20
00:04:01,297 --> 00:04:13,798
Property 3. The covariance of a sum with a sum splits into pairs. Each term on the left goes with each term on the right. Two terms times two terms give four covariances.

21
00:04:14,248 --> 00:04:23,976
One more fact follows from the definition. The order does not matter: the covariance of Y and X equals the covariance of X and Y.

22
00:04:24,426 --> 00:04:38,250
Section 2. Question type C asks for the variance of a sum or a difference, when the variables can be related. Method card C has five steps. The grid shows them for a U plus b V.

23
00:04:38,700 --> 00:04:45,463
Step 1: write the variance as the covariance of the quantity with itself. This is property 1.

24
00:04:45,913 --> 00:04:55,214
Step 2: expand. Each term on the left goes with each term on the right. This is property 3. The grid has one cell for each pair.

25
00:04:55,664 --> 00:05:05,285
Step 3: move the factors out. The covariance of a U with b V is a b times the covariance of U and V. This is property 2.

26
00:05:05,735 --> 00:05:15,549
Step 4: the diagonal cells are variances, by property 1. The two cells off the diagonal are equal, because the order does not matter.

27
00:05:15,999 --> 00:05:22,057
Step 5: add the cells. If the variables are independent, their covariance is 0.

28
00:05:22,507 --> 00:05:29,313
The slide asks an exercise: what is the variance of X plus Y? Pause the video and try it.

29
00:05:29,763 --> 00:05:37,933
Step 1 of card C: by property 1, the variance of X plus Y is the covariance of X plus Y with itself.

30
00:05:38,383 --> 00:05:51,802
Steps 2 and 3: by property 3, the grid has four cells: covariance of X with X, X with Y, Y with X, and Y with Y. All factors are 1, so step 3 changes nothing.

31
00:05:52,252 --> 00:06:01,148
Step 4: the diagonal cells are the variances. The two other cells are equal, so together they give 2 times the covariance.

32
00:06:01,598 --> 00:06:09,321
Step 5: when X and Y are independent, the covariance is 0. Then we get property 2 of the variance.

33
00:06:09,771 --> 00:06:30,997
For the 5 people, the variances are 87344.4 and 16305.36, and the covariance is 26672.6. The result is 156994.96.

34
00:06:31,447 --> 00:06:46,935
Section 3. For observed pairs, the sample covariance is 1 over n minus 1, times the sum of y i minus y bar, times x i minus x bar. Now we treat the 5 people as a sample.

35
00:06:47,385 --> 00:06:59,076
x bar is 4093 and y bar is 1406.8. Here are the deviations. Each set of deviations adds to 0.

36
00:06:59,526 --> 00:07:05,265
Multiply the two deviations of each person. These are the signed rectangles again.

37
00:07:05,715 --> 00:07:12,691
The products are the same as before. Their sum is 133363.

38
00:07:13,141 --> 00:07:30,997
Now divide by n minus 1, which is 4, not by 5. The sample covariance is 33340.75. It is larger than 26672.6 only because of the divisor.

39
00:07:31,447 --> 00:07:33,985
Now all 236 people.

40
00:07:34,435 --> 00:07:47,491
The purple lines are x bar, 3637.864, and y bar, 1284.263. They make four quadrants.

41
00:07:47,941 --> 00:07:53,893
Blue points have a positive product of deviations. Red points have a negative product.

42
00:07:54,343 --> 00:08:03,602
Quadrants 1 and 3 have 91 and 97 people. Quadrants 2 and 4 have 24 each. So most products are positive.

43
00:08:04,052 --> 00:08:17,556
The sum of the products in each quadrant is shown. The positive sums are much larger. The total is S x y, 7993433.4.

44
00:08:18,006 --> 00:08:30,443
Divide by n minus 1, which is 235. The sample covariance is 34014.61, in grams times cubic centimeters.

45
00:08:30,893 --> 00:08:49,901
Section 4. The covariance has a problem: it depends on the units. In grams and cubic centimeters it is 34014.61. In kilograms and litres it is 0.034. The data is the same. Only the number changed.

46
00:08:50,351 --> 00:09:06,757
The correlation coefficient rho divides the covariance by the two standard deviations. The units cancel. So the correlation has no unit, and it is the same in both unit systems: 0.7937.

47
00:09:07,207 --> 00:09:21,607
For data, we use three sums. S x y is the sum of the products of deviations. S x x is the sum of squared deviations of x. S y y is the sum of squared deviations of y.

48
00:09:22,057 --> 00:09:33,257
Line 1: the sample correlation r puts the sample covariance over the two sample standard deviations. Each one has a factor 1 over n minus 1.

49
00:09:33,707 --> 00:09:43,136
Line 2: the two square roots multiply. The square root of 1 over n minus 1, squared, is 1 over n minus 1.

50
00:09:43,586 --> 00:10:01,293
Line 3: the factor 1 over n minus 1 cancels with the numerator. So r equals S x y over the square root of S x x times S y y. The sums S x x and S x y come back in every regression lecture.

51
00:10:01,743 --> 00:10:14,031
Correlation measures the strength of a linear relationship. r is always between minus 1 and 1. Here the points are close to a rising line. r is 0.96.

52
00:10:14,481 --> 00:10:21,350
The brainhead data has r equals 0.79: a clear rising trend, with more spread.

53
00:10:21,800 --> 00:10:25,811
Here there is no linear trend. r is small: 0.12.

54
00:10:26,261 --> 00:10:31,914
Here the points fall from left to right. r is negative: minus 0.79.

55
00:10:32,364 --> 00:10:58,284
Now calculate r for the 5 people. S x y is 133363. S x x is 436722. S y y is 81526.8. So r is 133363 over the square root of their product. That is 0.7068.

56
00:10:58,734 --> 00:11:26,297
For all 236 people, S x y is 7993433.4, S x x is 30647233.7, and S y y is 3309753.7. So r is 0.7937. This is a strong positive linear relationship.

57
00:11:26,747 --> 00:11:36,837
Question type D gives data, or computer output, and asks for the sample covariance or the correlation. Method card D has five steps.

58
00:11:37,287 --> 00:11:40,274
Step 1: the means, x bar and y bar.

59
00:11:40,724 --> 00:11:45,972
Step 2: the deviations of x and of y. Each column adds to 0.

60
00:11:46,422 --> 00:11:59,777
Step 3: the three sums. S x y is the sum of the products of the deviations. S x x and S y y are the sums of the squares. Computer output often gives these sums.

61
00:12:00,227 --> 00:12:05,304
Step 4: the sample covariance is S x y over n minus 1.

62
00:12:05,754 --> 00:12:19,194
Step 5: r is S x y over the square root of S x x times S y y. Check that r is between minus 1 and 1, and that its sign is the sign of S x y.

63
00:12:19,644 --> 00:12:31,527
A type D question with the data of Tutorial 1, question 1. Pause the video and try it. Find the sample covariance and the correlation of machine hours and cost.

64
00:12:31,977 --> 00:12:46,355
Step 1 of card D: the hours add to 707, so x bar is 101. The costs add to 189, so y bar is 27. The purple lines mark the means.

65
00:12:46,805 --> 00:12:59,563
Step 2: the deviations. In row 1, 110 minus 101 is plus 9, and 26 minus 27 is minus 1. Each column adds to 0.

66
00:13:00,013 --> 00:13:11,959
Step 3: square the deviations. The x squares add to S x x, 1294. The y squares add to S y y, 322.

67
00:13:12,409 --> 00:13:31,353
Still step 3: multiply the two deviations in each row. In row 1, plus 9 times minus 1 is minus 9. The products add to S x y, minus 531. The rectangles show the products: most are red, so the sum is negative.

68
00:13:31,803 --> 00:13:39,739
Step 4: the sample covariance is minus 531 over 6, which is minus 88.5.

69
00:13:40,189 --> 00:14:13,293
Step 5: r is minus 531 over the square root of 1294 times 322. 1294 times 322 is 416668, and its square root is 645.50. So r is minus 0.8226. The bars show the ratio. r is negative, like S x y, and between minus 1 and 1.

70
00:14:13,743 --> 00:14:29,273
A type D question from computer output. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 1, question 2, gives R output for 100 aircraft. Find the sample covariance and the correlation of flight hours and cost.

71
00:14:29,723 --> 00:14:40,582
Step 1 of card D: the output gives the means, 148.8 and 1822. R did steps 2 and 3 for us.

72
00:14:41,032 --> 00:14:59,741
Step 3: read the three sums. S x x is 731093. S y y is 47854282. S x y is 4686071.

73
00:15:00,191 --> 00:15:12,970
Step 4: the sample covariance is 4686071 over 99, which is 47334.05.

74
00:15:13,420 --> 00:15:41,516
Step 5: the square root of 731093 times 47854282 is 5914890.6. So r is 4686071 over 5914890.6, which is 0.7922.

75
00:15:41,966 --> 00:15:50,307
Check: r is positive, like S x y, and between minus 1 and 1. The blue bar is shorter than the gray bar.

76
00:15:50,757 --> 00:16:01,040
A practice question with the summaries of all 236 people. Pause the video and try it. Find the sample variance of x minus y.

77
00:16:01,490 --> 00:16:08,850
Step 1 of card C: the variance of X minus Y is the covariance of X minus Y with itself.

78
00:16:09,300 --> 00:16:21,652
Steps 2 and 3: write X minus Y as X plus minus 1 times Y. The grid has four cells. Each covariance cell off the diagonal has the factor minus 1.

79
00:16:22,102 --> 00:16:29,654
Step 4: the diagonal cells are the variances. The two other cells give minus 2 times the covariance.

80
00:16:30,104 --> 00:16:51,949
Step 5: put in the sample values: 130413.76 plus 14084.06 minus 2 times 34014.61. The sample versions follow the same rules, because all three use the divisor n minus 1.

81
00:16:52,399 --> 00:17:16,165
130413.76 plus 14084.06 is 144497.82. 2 times 34014.61 is 68029.22. The result is 76468.60.

82
00:17:16,615 --> 00:17:24,252
The last practice question. Pause the video and try it. Find S x y from the sample covariance, then r.

83
00:17:24,702 --> 00:17:36,478
Card D, step 4 backwards: the sample covariance is S x y over n minus 1. So S x y is n minus 1 times the sample covariance.

84
00:17:36,928 --> 00:17:51,563
n minus 1 is 235. 235 times 34014.61 is 7993433.35.

85
00:17:52,013 --> 00:18:19,895
Step 5: the square root of S x x times S y y is 10071484. So r is 7993433.35 over 10071484, which is 0.7937. The small difference from the S x y of the data comes from the rounding of the sample covariance.

86
00:18:20,345 --> 00:18:41,145
To recap. Card C finds the variance of a sum or a difference: write it as a covariance with itself, expand the grid, move the factors out, use the variances on the diagonal, and add. The variance of X plus or minus Y has the term plus or minus 2 times the covariance.

87
00:18:41,595 --> 00:19:03,611
Card D computes the sample covariance, S x y over n minus 1, and the correlation r, S x y over the square root of S x x times S y y. r has no unit, and it is between minus 1 and 1. The next video reviews four distributions, and the idea of a regression line.
